---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    You are a finite system in a world that is not. Everything you know about it
    arrived through channels that discard almost all of it, and was compressed
    again into a model small enough to act on. That compression is what your
    intelligence <em>is</em>. It is also why there are things you cannot notice
    you are missing.
  </p>

  <h2>What the course argues</h2>
  <p>
    Intelligence is not a quantity that some animals have more of. It is a set
    of trades, made under a hard constraint, and every one of them buys a
    capability by giving something up. We start with a bacterium that holds
    nothing at all and work upward, and by the end the account is detailed
    enough to turn on you.
  </p>
  <p>
    The last weeks are the point of the earlier ones. Once you can say what a
    desert ant cannot detect about its own navigation, the same question about
    your own judgement stops being rhetorical — and becomes much harder, because
    you are asking it from inside the thing you are asking about.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will read primary literature every week, most of it outside whatever
    discipline you came from. Four fields are in play and none of them is
    optional: evolutionary biology says when a capacity appeared and how often,
    neuroscience says what implements it, psychology says how it was measured,
    and philosophy asks whether the concept survives being pressed on. A claim
    supported by only one of them is incomplete, and you will be asked to say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Much of the field's best-known findings have not replicated. You will grade
    evidence rather than accumulate it, and you will do this on claims that are
    still being argued about, with no answer key. In week 2 you will write down
    what you currently think intelligence is, before you know anything, and in
    week 11 you will revise it and account for what moved.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students from any of biology, neuroscience, psychology,
    philosophy or computer science. You need no background in the other three,
    but you do need to be willing to read outside your own — most weeks, the
    evidence that settles a question comes from a field you have not studied.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no prerequisite and no exam. If you want a semester of established
    results, this is the wrong course; roughly a third of what we cover is
    contested, and the disagreements are the content rather than an
    inconvenience.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, and what happens in each.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces of work, what each is for, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The lecture programme, with slides where they exist.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this and what to ask each of them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-border);
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
